WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:16.187 --> 00:00:17.290
Look at that.

00:00:17.290 --> 00:00:21.393
That is the biggest crowd
MTV has ever, ever had.

00:00:22.620 --> 00:00:24.090
Why?

00:00:24.090 --> 00:00:26.063
Two words, Backstreet Boys.

00:00:27.740 --> 00:00:29.160
In the '90s, it was a great time

00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:31.388
to be in the music business.

00:00:31.388 --> 00:00:32.280
Things were booming.

00:00:32.280 --> 00:00:37.150
And as a result, expensive
parties, big launches.

00:00:37.150 --> 00:00:38.900
Yeah, that was part of the culture.

00:00:41.720 --> 00:00:44.650
This was the era of NSYNC
and the Backstreet Boys.

00:00:44.650 --> 00:00:46.310
This was the era where a single hit

00:00:46.310 --> 00:00:48.393
could sell you 10 million albums plus.

00:00:49.750 --> 00:00:51.303
They were just printing money.

00:00:53.680 --> 00:00:57.800
You had the great sales
of CDs over that period

00:00:57.800 --> 00:01:01.283
dwarfing whatever had happened
in the industry before.

00:01:02.600 --> 00:01:04.453
The profits coming in were massive.

00:01:05.990 --> 00:01:08.530
It was a hell of a lot of fun.

00:01:08.530 --> 00:01:10.550
From my viewpoint, we were gonna get

00:01:10.550 --> 00:01:12.143
better and better all the time.

00:01:18.544 --> 00:01:23.044
We really didn't foresee the
change in the technology.

00:01:26.650 --> 00:01:28.685
We'll be telling you
tonight about a method

00:01:28.685 --> 00:01:32.783
of compressing audio data called MP3.

00:01:36.160 --> 00:01:38.853
And that was the end
of everything we know.

00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:50.300
The birth of the MP3 was a seminal moment

00:01:50.300 --> 00:01:52.620
for all of media and entertainment.

00:01:52.620 --> 00:01:55.350
It would completely
change the music industry.

00:01:55.350 --> 00:01:57.140
Completely disrupt it.

00:01:57.140 --> 00:02:01.373
It opened up people's minds
to what the future would be.

00:02:04.509 --> 00:02:06.037
Don't steal our music!

00:02:06.037 --> 00:02:08.290
Its music is free for downloading.

00:02:08.290 --> 00:02:10.520
The music industry is not viable.

00:02:10.520 --> 00:02:14.260
Movies, books, television,
nothing creative

00:02:14.260 --> 00:02:15.803
has any value anymore.

00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:20.410
The music industry simply did not embrace

00:02:20.410 --> 00:02:24.090
the magnitude of the change and it failed.

00:02:24.090 --> 00:02:25.510
You can't fight progress.

00:02:25.510 --> 00:02:27.323
You can't fight technology.

00:02:30.600 --> 00:02:33.830
The MP3 totally changed
people's relationship

00:02:33.830 --> 00:02:37.663
to the internet, to culture,
to how society works.

00:02:38.510 --> 00:02:41.030
It's a fact that was so much bigger than

00:02:41.030 --> 00:02:42.343
just the music industry.

00:03:16.296 --> 00:03:18.810
Would you say that you love audio?

00:03:18.810 --> 00:03:19.850
You just love it?

00:03:19.850 --> 00:03:21.663
Oh sure, oh sure.

00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:26.290
I have to admit I got older,
so I'm not as critical

00:03:26.290 --> 00:03:30.450
in listening as I've
been some decades ago,

00:03:30.450 --> 00:03:34.683
but I definitely love
music and love audio.

00:03:35.800 --> 00:03:38.380
And I have to say with all this great

00:03:38.380 --> 00:03:41.720
reproduction technology, the best is still

00:03:41.720 --> 00:03:45.223
to sit in a concert hall
to hear real musicians.

00:03:54.320 --> 00:03:57.480
When people call me the father of MP3,

00:03:57.480 --> 00:04:01.377
I always have to add, "No, no,
I was, as you sometimes say,

00:04:01.377 --> 00:04:05.610
"standing on the shoulders of giants."

00:04:05.610 --> 00:04:07.710
But my original contribution is really

00:04:07.710 --> 00:04:09.803
some of the basic ideas to MP3.

00:04:10.877 --> 00:04:14.010
The story of the MP3 really
begins in West Germany

00:04:14.010 --> 00:04:18.113
back then in the early
'80s and even late '70s.

00:04:20.140 --> 00:04:24.100
People started trying to compress music,

00:04:24.100 --> 00:04:28.033
including my own thesis
adviser, Dieter Seitzer.

00:04:29.350 --> 00:04:32.250
Dieter Seitzer was a visionary
who came up with the MP3.

00:04:33.460 --> 00:04:36.210
He was seeing the original
digitization of music

00:04:36.210 --> 00:04:38.470
with a compact disc and thinking,

00:04:38.470 --> 00:04:40.830
instead of manufacturing
millions upon millions

00:04:40.830 --> 00:04:44.040
of plastic discs, why
don't we store all the data

00:04:44.040 --> 00:04:47.140
in one centralized
computer and then stream it

00:04:47.140 --> 00:04:48.643
to people in their homes.

00:04:49.680 --> 00:04:53.730
So he applied for a patent
to use the phone system

00:04:53.730 --> 00:04:55.193
to transmit music.

00:04:56.090 --> 00:04:58.883
1982, that's when they
first had this idea.

00:04:59.900 --> 00:05:01.473
But they had a huge problem.

00:05:02.750 --> 00:05:04.920
The compact disc took
about a million bytes

00:05:04.920 --> 00:05:08.340
of information to store
one second of audio.

00:05:08.340 --> 00:05:10.440
And the pipe they were
trying to send it through,

00:05:10.440 --> 00:05:13.733
the telephone, could only
do about 128,000 bytes.

00:05:15.060 --> 00:05:17.840
For this to work, they somehow
had to magically shrink

00:05:17.840 --> 00:05:21.850
the size of the compact
disc by 90% or more.

00:05:21.850 --> 00:05:24.717
The patent examiner looked at it and said,

00:05:24.717 --> 00:05:27.497
"No, it's not possible."

00:05:29.360 --> 00:05:32.060
Essentially, almost everyone
thought this was impossible.

00:05:32.060 --> 00:05:33.593
There's no way to do this.

00:05:38.140 --> 00:05:41.810
But around '87 or '88, Dieter Seitzer's

00:05:41.810 --> 00:05:44.190
brilliant graduate student, Brandenburg,

00:05:44.190 --> 00:05:47.680
made a breakthrough by taking advantage

00:05:47.680 --> 00:05:50.073
of this sort of obscure
academic discipline.

00:05:51.260 --> 00:05:52.710
Psychoacoustics.

00:05:58.180 --> 00:06:01.343
The human ear has a
lot of inherited flaws.

00:06:02.290 --> 00:06:04.360
For example, two tones
that are close together

00:06:04.360 --> 00:06:07.260
in pitch will begin to
cancel each other out.

00:06:07.260 --> 00:06:09.693
And therefore he realized
much of the information

00:06:09.693 --> 00:06:12.050
in the compact disc was irrelevant.

00:06:12.050 --> 00:06:14.033
The ear couldn't actually hear it.

00:06:15.490 --> 00:06:18.110
So he devises a series
of mathematical models

00:06:18.110 --> 00:06:21.200
to eliminate all of those
inefficient bits of data

00:06:21.200 --> 00:06:23.240
that you will never hear.

00:06:23.240 --> 00:06:25.950
And in so doing, he was
in fact able to shrink

00:06:25.950 --> 00:06:28.093
the music by an extraordinary amount.

00:06:44.798 --> 00:06:46.610
What was done in the end

00:06:46.610 --> 00:06:49.407
exceeded the expectations of everybody.

00:07:17.260 --> 00:07:18.780
The functionality of what they put up

00:07:18.780 --> 00:07:20.490
was relatively limited.

00:07:20.490 --> 00:07:23.290
You had to go and get a
serial number where you paid

00:07:23.290 --> 00:07:26.230
and licensed it to unlock
full functionality.

00:07:26.230 --> 00:07:28.570
But the way the software
underground worked

00:07:28.570 --> 00:07:30.910
in the '90s is that, practically,

00:07:30.910 --> 00:07:33.323
these serial numbers
were available anywhere.

00:07:34.500 --> 00:07:37.957
We tried writing, "Look,
this is stolen software.

00:07:37.957 --> 00:07:41.724
"We will send the public
prosecutor after you."

00:07:41.724 --> 00:07:46.724
Which of course doesn't
really work these days.

00:07:50.080 --> 00:07:53.240
It starts to percolate
out of the academic audio

00:07:53.240 --> 00:07:55.623
and computing sphere
into the broader public.

00:07:56.800 --> 00:07:59.633
And by '96, the pirates find out about it.

00:08:09.100 --> 00:08:12.133
The year's about 1997, 1998.

00:08:13.140 --> 00:08:14.673
I'm in seventh grade.

00:08:15.510 --> 00:08:18.667
A school friend of mine
said, "Hey there's a way

00:08:18.667 --> 00:08:21.367
"you can listen to music
for free on your computer.

00:08:21.367 --> 00:08:22.487
"Check this out."

00:08:23.860 --> 00:08:25.660
After I discovered my first MP3,

00:08:25.660 --> 00:08:28.960
it blew the floodgates wide open.

00:08:28.960 --> 00:08:33.080
That's all I did every single
day, every single night.

00:08:40.060 --> 00:08:42.810
I was the founder of the MP3 piracy group

00:08:42.810 --> 00:08:44.373
known as Audio Punks.

00:08:45.460 --> 00:08:47.620
We specialized in
everything from punk rock,

00:08:47.620 --> 00:08:50.453
to ska, to hardcore and heavy metal.

00:08:52.940 --> 00:08:56.300
We had downloaders from
Indonesia, Japan, or Russia,

00:08:56.300 --> 00:08:58.243
you name it, people are downloading.

00:09:01.980 --> 00:09:05.530
Back then in the late '90s,
if you wanted to find MP3s,

00:09:05.530 --> 00:09:07.263
you would go into IRC channels.

00:09:08.710 --> 00:09:10.690
It was a series of chat rooms that were

00:09:10.690 --> 00:09:12.883
hidden and invite-only.

00:09:14.110 --> 00:09:17.510
I eventually found these
great little channels

00:09:17.510 --> 00:09:22.190
that have membership solid
by enforcing a upload ratio

00:09:22.190 --> 00:09:24.820
where you had to upload
three songs, or two songs

00:09:24.820 --> 00:09:27.320
or whatever number, in
order to download one song.

00:09:29.000 --> 00:09:31.060
So I would pool money
together with my friends

00:09:31.060 --> 00:09:32.823
in order to get a CD.

00:09:33.710 --> 00:09:37.993
Rip a CD, and then
compress that into an MP3.

00:09:39.040 --> 00:09:42.150
And then we would spend all
night uploading the album,

00:09:42.150 --> 00:09:43.700
putting it out on the internet.

00:09:45.130 --> 00:09:47.950
We were eventually able to
keep repeating the process

00:09:47.950 --> 00:09:50.733
and have almost an
assembly line structure.

00:09:51.890 --> 00:09:54.260
And in our prime, we had probably close to

00:09:54.260 --> 00:09:56.143
a thousand albums in our catalog.

00:09:58.440 --> 00:10:01.350
Man, it was almost like a full-time job

00:10:01.350 --> 00:10:04.313
just dedicated to these song files.

00:10:06.760 --> 00:10:08.420
Did you ever think like,

00:10:08.420 --> 00:10:12.040
wow, this is kinda like I'm
stealing from these artists.

00:10:12.040 --> 00:10:13.390
Did that ever occur to you?

00:10:14.700 --> 00:10:17.810
You know, in the late '90s we were idiots.

00:10:17.810 --> 00:10:18.840
We were kids.

00:10:18.840 --> 00:10:21.070
You know, I started this when I was 12.

00:10:21.070 --> 00:10:22.620
I didn't know what I was doing.

00:10:23.630 --> 00:10:25.593
It wasn't called piracy back then.

00:10:26.450 --> 00:10:27.943
It was just file sharing.

00:10:48.450 --> 00:10:51.430
In the late '90s, my
staff started monitoring

00:10:51.430 --> 00:10:54.670
the online space and we really did see

00:10:54.670 --> 00:10:58.760
a significant amount of
interest in MP3 all of a sudden

00:10:58.760 --> 00:11:02.173
because of its ability
to compress a music file.

00:11:05.120 --> 00:11:09.150
The latest advance, called
MP3, comes via the internet.

00:11:09.150 --> 00:11:11.670
It's the future of music and audio.

00:11:11.670 --> 00:11:13.720
I'm sure that there are
millions of you out there

00:11:13.720 --> 00:11:15.720
who not only understand what digital

00:11:15.720 --> 00:11:17.800
downloading technology is about,

00:11:17.800 --> 00:11:20.823
but who use it daily to
great personal advantage.

00:11:22.310 --> 00:11:24.010
I think there really wasn't any sort

00:11:24.010 --> 00:11:26.513
of panic in the industry
in the early days.

00:11:27.560 --> 00:11:31.540
My team did look at it
and see it as not really

00:11:31.540 --> 00:11:34.250
as much of a threat as an opportunity.

00:11:34.250 --> 00:11:36.813
We used to call it the celestial jukebox.

00:11:38.210 --> 00:11:40.230
Celestial jukebox was a theoretical

00:11:40.230 --> 00:11:41.373
construct at the time.

00:11:42.380 --> 00:11:45.000
Every song ever made was gonna be

00:11:45.000 --> 00:11:47.200
in what we call the cloud right now,

00:11:47.200 --> 00:11:49.000
and would be instantaneously available

00:11:49.000 --> 00:11:51.060
to anyone on the planet simply by

00:11:51.060 --> 00:11:52.723
pressing a button on your gizmo.

00:11:54.160 --> 00:11:58.663
But the industry really did
not embrace that opportunity.

00:12:01.600 --> 00:12:04.437
I think when we first heard the term MP3

00:12:04.437 --> 00:12:08.000
we didn't really know
if that was important

00:12:08.000 --> 00:12:09.840
or how it might be important.

00:12:09.840 --> 00:12:12.853
And even if it was important,
how'd it affect us?

00:12:16.580 --> 00:12:19.760
But there was an awareness
that the digital revolution

00:12:19.760 --> 00:12:23.575
was coming and anybody who
was in a senior position

00:12:23.575 --> 00:12:26.373
had to say how do we
make this work for us?

00:12:27.890 --> 00:12:32.300
We began trying to find
a way to legitimately

00:12:32.300 --> 00:12:33.983
sell music on the internet.

00:12:34.860 --> 00:12:36.330
What systems do we develop?

00:12:36.330 --> 00:12:37.980
How do we deal with how we sell it?

00:12:37.980 --> 00:12:39.070
How do you price it?

00:12:39.070 --> 00:12:41.860
Sony Music said today it'll be the first

00:12:41.860 --> 00:12:43.740
major recording company to sell

00:12:43.740 --> 00:12:45.830
new releases through the internet.

00:12:45.830 --> 00:12:47.240
These virtual singles will be released

00:12:47.240 --> 00:12:50.420
at the same time as
CDs go into the stores.

00:12:50.420 --> 00:12:52.450
We were working at it.

00:12:52.450 --> 00:12:55.380
But the senior executives, they still had

00:12:55.380 --> 00:12:59.220
the mentality of you needed a hard good.

00:12:59.220 --> 00:13:01.353
And that was the basis of their business.

00:13:03.350 --> 00:13:04.780
The music industry was making

00:13:04.780 --> 00:13:06.630
gobs of money selling CDs.

00:13:06.630 --> 00:13:07.780
That's the bottom line.

00:13:08.620 --> 00:13:10.610
So companies like mine, mp3.com,

00:13:10.610 --> 00:13:11.457
that went to them and said,

00:13:11.457 --> 00:13:13.597
"Hey, we wanna work with you.

00:13:13.597 --> 00:13:14.917
"Let's figure out something that gives

00:13:14.917 --> 00:13:17.260
"the consumer some value
and gives you some value."

00:13:17.260 --> 00:13:19.480
They didn't want to hear any of that.

00:13:19.480 --> 00:13:22.137
They tell me, "No, the computer is for

00:13:22.137 --> 00:13:24.827
"word processing and spreadsheets."

00:13:25.710 --> 00:13:26.760
They couldn't see it.

00:13:28.400 --> 00:13:30.430
We had meeting after meeting after meeting

00:13:30.430 --> 00:13:32.020
with companies with proposals for new

00:13:32.020 --> 00:13:33.453
technological ideas.

00:13:35.000 --> 00:13:36.860
Generally who knew absolutely nothing

00:13:36.860 --> 00:13:38.520
about how the record business works,

00:13:38.520 --> 00:13:40.720
which made it difficult
for them to express

00:13:40.720 --> 00:13:42.223
how it was good for us.

00:13:44.640 --> 00:13:48.217
So basically all of this
stuff was for naught.

00:13:50.568 --> 00:13:53.860
And then peer-to-peer came
along and changed everything.

00:14:07.480 --> 00:14:08.620
I just stopped going to school.

00:14:08.620 --> 00:14:09.670
I left my dorm room.

00:14:09.670 --> 00:14:10.820
I didn't tell any of my friends.

00:14:10.820 --> 00:14:11.870
I just stopped going.

00:14:14.240 --> 00:14:16.240
I knew that if I had told them what I was

00:14:16.240 --> 00:14:17.400
planning on doing, they would have

00:14:17.400 --> 00:14:18.910
convinced me not to do it.

00:14:18.910 --> 00:14:21.460
Or they would have questioned
my decision to leave.

00:14:23.830 --> 00:14:28.360
In 1999, observing that
file sharing was fun,

00:14:28.360 --> 00:14:29.683
but was not easy,

00:14:30.850 --> 00:14:32.940
Shawn Fanning and Sean Parker come around

00:14:32.940 --> 00:14:34.683
and create Napster.

00:14:37.170 --> 00:14:39.260
They were two teenagers hanging around

00:14:39.260 --> 00:14:42.313
an IRC chat channel swapping MP3 files.

00:14:43.300 --> 00:14:45.150
Now at the time, there
were all these MP3 files

00:14:45.150 --> 00:14:46.720
being created, but there was no sort of

00:14:46.720 --> 00:14:49.923
central index where you could
go download them easily.

00:14:50.950 --> 00:14:53.027
And so Fanning said, "Why don't I create

00:14:53.027 --> 00:14:55.517
"a new technology that will connect

00:14:55.517 --> 00:14:57.770
"all these files together?"

00:14:57.770 --> 00:14:59.973
Something called a peer-to-peer server.

00:15:00.970 --> 00:15:03.680
My computers appear, and
your computers appear.

00:15:03.680 --> 00:15:05.280
The files that are on your computer,

00:15:05.280 --> 00:15:07.543
I have access to them and vice versa.

00:15:09.060 --> 00:15:12.540
So it was like the
ultimate file trading tool.

00:15:12.540 --> 00:15:14.422
And it facilitated really low friction

00:15:14.422 --> 00:15:15.833
sharing of music.

00:15:16.810 --> 00:15:18.250
Parker's role was essentially

00:15:18.250 --> 00:15:19.993
the face of the organization.

00:15:21.010 --> 00:15:22.580
Fanning wrote the code.

00:15:22.580 --> 00:15:24.420
Fanning kind of did all the technical

00:15:24.420 --> 00:15:25.703
and computer side stuff.

00:15:32.060 --> 00:15:34.240
I was spending two or three days in a row

00:15:34.240 --> 00:15:35.950
sitting at a machine writing code.

00:15:35.950 --> 00:15:38.730
I'd pass out on the desk or on the floor.

00:15:38.730 --> 00:15:39.913
It was just a blur.

00:15:41.900 --> 00:15:43.950
This is a guy who just sat in the basement

00:15:43.950 --> 00:15:46.003
and cranked for six months.

00:15:46.940 --> 00:15:49.140
It was the first computer
program he ever wrote.

00:15:49.140 --> 00:15:51.940
He went out and bought
a Visual Basic book,

00:15:51.940 --> 00:15:54.440
knew what he wanted to get
to and just kept at it.

00:15:55.970 --> 00:15:57.793
He was not to be deterred.

00:15:59.410 --> 00:16:01.653
And, of course, it took off.

00:16:09.640 --> 00:16:11.500
My head of anti-piracy, Frank Creighton,

00:16:11.500 --> 00:16:13.217
came into my office and
said, "I've just found

00:16:13.217 --> 00:16:15.407
"the most fascinating thing."

00:16:17.040 --> 00:16:18.163
It was Napster.

00:16:19.530 --> 00:16:23.187
I put in, you know, Madonna's "Holiday."

00:16:25.250 --> 00:16:28.150
I was like, "Whoa, that's pretty amazing."

00:16:28.150 --> 00:16:30.010
That's like the celestial jukebox.

00:16:30.010 --> 00:16:32.263
We've been talking about this for years.

00:16:33.210 --> 00:16:36.997
We immediately contacted them and said,

00:16:36.997 --> 00:16:38.487
"Ya know, really cool system, but you're

00:16:38.487 --> 00:16:40.407
"infringing on copyright."

00:16:40.407 --> 00:16:43.597
"Well, if you send us a
list of all of your songs

00:16:43.597 --> 00:16:46.350
"we'll make sure that none
of them are on there."

00:16:46.350 --> 00:16:47.737
I said, "All right, just start with

00:16:47.737 --> 00:16:49.527
"the Billboard Top 200 songs.

00:16:49.527 --> 00:16:51.080
"Take those off."

00:16:51.080 --> 00:16:53.070
They said, "Okay, fine, we'll try that."

00:16:53.070 --> 00:16:54.950
Well, they didn't.

00:16:54.950 --> 00:16:56.750
And it just grew and grew and grew.

00:16:56.750 --> 00:16:58.140
It's called Napster.

00:16:58.140 --> 00:16:59.860
And it's by far the fastest way

00:16:59.860 --> 00:17:01.900
to download music from the internet.

00:17:01.900 --> 00:17:03.300
It allows anyone to copy

00:17:03.300 --> 00:17:05.621
thousands of musical selections for free.

00:17:05.621 --> 00:17:07.030
And it's spread so quickly

00:17:07.030 --> 00:17:08.730
that it's clogging computer networks

00:17:08.730 --> 00:17:10.950
on hundreds of campuses.

00:17:10.950 --> 00:17:14.423
So this became hugely
popular overnight basically.

00:17:15.290 --> 00:17:18.343
It was really one of the first viral apps.

00:17:22.950 --> 00:17:27.000
The hysteria over Napster culminated in,

00:17:27.000 --> 00:17:31.133
I would say, an RIAA board
meeting in February of 2000.

00:17:35.290 --> 00:17:37.840
It was in Los Angeles at
the Four Seasons Hotel.

00:17:37.840 --> 00:17:39.433
I will never forget this day.

00:17:43.190 --> 00:17:44.760
All of the heads of the labels,

00:17:44.760 --> 00:17:47.367
literally the titans
of the music business,

00:17:47.367 --> 00:17:48.723
were in that room.

00:17:49.790 --> 00:17:52.970
I had somebody wheel in a
PC and put some speakers up

00:17:52.970 --> 00:17:56.210
and I started doing a name that tune.

00:17:56.210 --> 00:17:58.513
Give me your last five biggest hits.

00:17:59.750 --> 00:18:01.610
Every single song that somebody named

00:18:01.610 --> 00:18:03.053
was available on Napster.

00:18:05.750 --> 00:18:07.493
Well, give me some old ones, like what,

00:18:07.493 --> 00:18:09.653
you know, name older artists.

00:18:10.880 --> 00:18:11.823
That was on there.

00:18:14.820 --> 00:18:17.963
Okay, now tell me what
you're releasing next week.

00:18:19.180 --> 00:18:22.740
And sure enough, you
know, 20 of the top new

00:18:22.740 --> 00:18:26.003
yet to be released songs
were on there as well.

00:18:32.900 --> 00:18:34.840
We used to have this line
in the record business

00:18:34.840 --> 00:18:38.080
that there was sort of nothing
a good hit couldn't fix.

00:18:38.080 --> 00:18:41.030
There was no screw up a
good hit couldn't fix.

00:18:41.030 --> 00:18:43.080
There was no amount of
money lost on a deal

00:18:43.080 --> 00:18:45.953
that a good hit couldn't fix.

00:18:50.498 --> 00:18:52.406
I think that was the
moment when people said,

00:18:52.406 --> 00:18:56.397
"Ooh, maybe a good hit
can't fix this one."

